<Window xmlns="https://github.com/avaloniaui"
        xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
        xmlns:d="http://schemas.microsoft.com/expression/blend/2008"
        xmlns:mc="http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/markup-compatibility/2006"
        mc:Ignorable="d" d:DesignWidth="1100" d:DesignHeight="720"
        x:Class="RorolalaDesktop.MainWindow"
        Width="1100" Height="720"
        Title="Rorolala Desktop"
        ExtendClientAreaToDecorationsHint="True"
        WindowDecorations="BorderOnly">
    <!-- The frame is the program's own rather than the desktop's, and `BorderOnly` is how the desktop is
         told so: the toolkit asks for a window with no title bar of the desktop's, keeps the resize
         border, and leaves the whole of the top to this program. `Full` would ask the desktop for a
         title bar instead — and then draw its own close, maximize and minimize over this program's,
         which is the one thing a window that draws its own row must not have.

         The hint is what hides the title bar on a display server that leaves the choice to the program:
         X11 and Wayland both draw one unless they are told not to, and the request only takes effect on
         a window that extended into its decorations.

         The top row is one line: the menu, then the window's own buttons. Its height is the height the
         decorations are told to reserve, which the window says in code rather than here — a window that is
         one dock gives its row the height the strip of tabs would have taken, since it has none — and the
         look is what draws the row and the buttons. -->
    <DockPanel>
        <!-- The row's own surface, and what the row is made of: the look fills it with the elevated ground and
             draws the one line along its foot that separates the row from the dock area below (Section 10). It
             is the container rather than a layer beside the menu, because a sibling of the menu has no width to
             be drawn in — the menu takes what is left of the row, and a border next to it would be a surface of
             no pixels, which is a line nobody can see.

             It carries no element role, and that is deliberate rather than left out: `ElementRole.TitleBar` is
             not a name for the row but a non-client hit test — the whole of the element's bounds is taken as the
             window's drag area, whatever is drawn inside it — so a row carrying it would answer for its own
             menu, and a press on a menu item would move the window instead of opening the menu. What a press
             here reaches is the window's own handler, and the drag area the framework reads is the one the
             decorations theme draws. -->
        <Border x:Name="TitleBar"
                Classes="title-bar"
                DockPanel.Dock="Top">
            <!-- The room the window's own buttons take is reserved by the row's own padding, which the look
                 states, rather than by laying them out here: the frame draws them over the row, so a button
                 that was a child of it would be a second one. What is left is the menu's. -->
            <Menu x:Name="MainMenu"
                  Classes="menu-bar"
                  Background="Transparent" />
        </Border>
        <ContentControl x:Name="DockAreaHost" />
    </DockPanel>
</Window>
